** To create a fresh chart 
- helm create <chart-name>
NOTE: the command will create a folder with the name of chart name and the folder containers
- Chart.yaml
- values.yaml
- .helmignore
- templates
- charts

.
├── charts
├── Chart.yaml
├── templates
│   ├── deployment.yaml
│   ├── _helpers.tpl
│   ├── hpa.yaml
│   ├── httproute.yaml
│   ├── ingress.yaml
│   ├── NOTES.txt
│   ├── serviceaccount.yaml
│   ├── service.yaml
│   └── tests
│       └── test-connection.yaml
└── values.yaml

1. .helmignore: Contains pattern to ignore when packaging helm charts
2. Chart.yaml: Contains all important chart information (chart metadata)

apiVersion: v2
name: kaif-chart
description: A Helm chart for Kubernetes
type: application
version: 0.1.0
appVersion: "1.16.0"
** NOTE: appVersion most of the time will be our Docker image tag version
https://helm.sh/docs/topics/charts

3. values.yaml: The default configuration values for this chart
4. charts folder: A directory containing any charts upon which this (parent chart) depends
  example: 
   - Parent chart: UMS App
   - Sub Charts (Chart Folder): mysql db chart
5. Template folder: 
    - A directory of templates that, when combined with values , will generate valid kubernetes manifest files.
    - It is where helm finds the YAML manifest for our Services, Deployments and other Kubernetes Objects.

6. templates/_helpers.tpl
    - Sometimes you want to create some reusable parts in your chart then we can use this file.
    - In the templates/ directory , any file that begins with an inderscore(_) is not expected to output a 
      Kubernetes manifest file.

7. templates/NOTES.txt
 - This is optional file which provides important information about our helm chart
 - Example: How to access the application deployed using helm chart

8. Test folder
   - We write tests that validate our chart works as expected when it is installed.
   - This tests also help the chart consumer understand that this helm chart is suppose to do.

9. README.md
   - to document about our chart



